 "paragraphs": [
  "Activities for Children",
  "Spring Bloom introduces the idea of place-value. The story helps illustrate how numbers are grouped in a base-ten system and what the ones, tens, and hundreds digits represent.",
  "In the story, Aliya and Zade use shells to represent ones, twigs to represent tens and pebbles to represent hundreds. Parents and teachers can encourage children to make the connections between shells (ones), twigs (tens), pebbles (hundreds) and the digits they represent. This can be done by discussing the relationships as they are encountered in the story, or after the story has been read. Children can use a place-value mat to keep track of the number of shells, twigs, and pebbles as they listen to the story. They can also play the following game as a way to enhance their understanding of the concept:",
  "Count Two Pebbles (Two-Player Game)",
  "Materials: Place-value mat, a pair of dice, shells, twigs, and pebbles (cutouts on following page).",
  "Directions: Give each player a place-value mat. Give each player 20 shells, 20 twigs, and 2 pebbles.",
  "The youngest player is Player One and goes first. Player One rolls the dice and adds the two numbers on the dice. Player One then places that number of shells on the place-value mat, and replaces every group of ten shells with a twig, when needed. Player One then hands the dice to Player Two, who repeats the same process. Every time a player has ten shells, he/she must remove the ten shells and replace them with a twig, and every time a player has ten twigs, he/she must remove the ten twigs and replace them with a pebble. The player to reach two pebbles first wins.",
  "Parents and teachers can encourage the players to periodically tell how many shells are represented if all pebbles and twigs are replaced with shells to help them make the connections between shells, twigs, pebbles, and ones, tens and hundreds."
 ],
